  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a thorough explanation of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n## What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\n## Do Women Have One Too?\n\nAdam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n## Why Is It Bigger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Starting point is the same:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth and those assigned female at birth until puberty.\n\n2. **Testosterone drives growth:** In men, the production of testosterone causes the larynx to grow larger, resulting in a deeper voice and a more prominent Adam's apple.\n\n3. **The larynx grows more in males:** Girls also undergo changes to their voice box during puberty, but the degree of laryngeal growth in females isn't as significant as in males, so most women don't have Adam's apples.\n\n4. **Shape differences also play a role:** There is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n5. **Cartilage positioning:** The structural size of the thyroid cartilage in males tends to increase during puberty, and the laryngeal protuberance becomes more anteriorly focused. In females, the laryngeal protuberance sits on the upper edge of the thyroid cartilage, and the larynx tends to be smaller in size, so the \"bump\" is much less visible or not discernible.\n\n## Why Does Voice Depth Matter?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one. This is why men generally have deeper voices than women.\n\n## Can Women Have a Prominent Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple. Conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can cause an increase in testosterone levels in women, potentially contributing to a more prominent laryngeal prominence.\n\nIn summary, the larger Adam's apple in men is primarily a result of testosterone-driven growth of the larynx during puberty, combined with differences in the angle and positioning of the thyroid cartilage between males and females.",
